From 3cb99ab13e1e42989889e15cc338bd6f6c6fff5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nieyin345 <nieyin0703@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:02:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(docs): write w:eastAsia so new docx files keep CJK
 typography

A docx built by Mcode left `w:rFonts/@w:eastAsia` unset. Word and our own
renderer then fall back to a script-based default for CJK, which is not
"missing glyphs" but a three-way mismatch inside a single paragraph:

  - CJK glyphs come from the system default (usually SimSun on Windows)
    while Latin glyphs come from Calibri;
  - line height is computed from the Latin metrics, so CJK characters
    overflow the leading;
  - the apparent weight differs between the two scripts.

This is the most direct cause of the "AI-edited Word files look ugly"
complaint, and one attribute removes it.

The font is chosen per platform, because each of these is only guaranteed to
exist on its own platform. Hard-coding one name means every file produced on
another platform references a font that is not installed there -- Word falls
back to the system default, which is exactly the previous behaviour plus a
misleading declaration.

The trade-off is deliberate: the same Mcode now produces byte-different docx
on Windows and macOS. Reproducibility gives way to "opens correctly on the
machine that produced it". No assertion compares bytes across platforms, and
none should.

Selection lives in the CLI (`packages/cli/src/formats/east-asia-font.ts`),
not in the renderer exporter: `exportDocxBytes` is a pure function and has no
business deciding which CJK fonts the calling machine has. It takes the font
as an optional parameter and omits `w:eastAsia` when absent, which keeps the
previous behaviour for every caller that does not pass one.
